00:00:54.935 --> 00:01:00.904 align:middle line:84%
She is the visiting lecturer--
visiting professor, visiting

00:01:00.904 --> 00:01:02.154 align:middle line:90%
professor--

00:01:02.154 --> 00:01:04.260 align:middle line:90%


00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:07.620 align:middle line:90%
here at GAMBIT.

00:01:07.620 --> 00:01:09.110 align:middle line:84%
Are you teaching something
in the spring?

00:01:09.110 --> 00:01:10.020 align:middle line:90%
GUEST SPEAKER: Yes.

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:11.940 align:middle line:90%
Cheating and ethics in games.

00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:14.090 align:middle line:84%
Brian can tell you
all about it.

00:01:14.090 --> 00:01:17.165 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: You can take this
as the sneak preview--

00:01:17.165 --> 00:01:18.135 align:middle line:90%
GUEST SPEAKER: Yes.

00:01:18.135 --> 00:01:21.530 align:middle line:84%
This is a taster of a little
bit of what we do.

00:01:21.530 --> 00:01:23.955 align:middle line:90%
Thanks.

00:01:23.955 --> 00:01:25.510 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:01:25.510 --> 00:01:29.250 align:middle line:84%
So it sounds like you all
have lots of opinions,

00:01:29.250 --> 00:01:31.190 align:middle line:90%
which will be good.

00:01:31.190 --> 00:01:34.959 align:middle line:84%
So to get started-- and
actually, I don't like the

00:01:34.959 --> 00:01:37.000 align:middle line:84%
term lecturer because I don't
get to lecture very much--

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:40.570 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to start by handing
around some index cards and I

00:01:40.570 --> 00:01:45.970 align:middle line:84%
want you to take a few minutes
and generate some examples of

00:01:45.970 --> 00:01:51.850 align:middle line:84%
ethical elements, procedures,
episodes, games, the more

00:01:51.850 --> 00:01:52.890 align:middle line:84%
specific you can
be the better.

00:01:52.890 --> 00:01:55.440 align:middle line:84%
And I'm actually going to
collect these at the end

00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:59.262 align:middle line:84%
because, as Phil mentioned, I'm
teaching this ethics and

00:01:59.262 --> 00:02:01.166 align:middle line:84%
games class and I want
to bring in more

00:02:01.166 --> 00:02:02.118 align:middle line:90%
examples of this stuff.

00:02:02.118 --> 00:02:04.498 align:middle line:84%
So this will actually
be useful for me.

00:02:04.498 --> 00:02:06.770 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Talking
about in games.

00:02:06.770 --> 00:02:07.580 align:middle line:90%
GUEST SPEAKER: Yeah.

00:02:07.580 --> 00:02:11.740 align:middle line:84%
So we were just talking about
Fable, but if you could think

00:02:11.740 --> 00:02:15.810 align:middle line:84%
of a more specific example from
it, a level, different

00:02:15.810 --> 00:02:19.195 align:middle line:84%
choices that you get with
characters in certain games,

00:02:19.195 --> 00:02:23.187 align:middle line:84%
whatever you can come up with
in terms of are there

00:02:23.187 --> 00:02:26.680 align:middle line:84%
interesting ethical dilemmas,
situations.

00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:27.678 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Wait.

00:02:27.678 --> 00:02:31.750 align:middle line:84%
Is this within the context of
the game, or are we talking

00:02:31.750 --> 00:02:34.373 align:middle line:84%
about in a multiplayer game
your conduct with other

00:02:34.373 --> 00:02:35.240 align:middle line:84%
players, and this
kind of thing?

00:02:35.240 --> 00:02:37.456 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Or, when I was in
Owen's office whether or not

00:02:37.456 --> 00:02:40.050 align:middle line:90%
to steal the swag.

00:02:40.050 --> 00:02:42.210 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: You can do it
within MMOs as well, or a

00:02:42.210 --> 00:02:45.660 align:middle line:90%
multiplayer game.

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:48.783 align:middle line:84%
You can continue to write
examples [UNINTELLIGIBLE] if

00:02:48.783 --> 00:02:50.226 align:middle line:90%
you can think of them.

00:02:50.226 --> 00:02:52.150 align:middle line:90%
But just to get you started--

00:02:52.150 --> 00:02:53.400 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:57.441 align:middle line:90%


00:02:57.441 --> 00:02:58.720 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: Well then
you can start by

00:02:58.720 --> 00:02:59.412 align:middle line:90%
giving me an example.

00:02:59.412 --> 00:03:00.755 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Sure.

00:03:00.755 --> 00:03:01.160 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:03:01.160 --> 00:03:02.800 align:middle line:90%
So I have a couple.

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:07.210 align:middle line:84%
Fighting games, obviously,
there's always kind of

00:03:07.210 --> 00:03:09.800 align:middle line:84%
questions and debate about
whether something's cheap or

00:03:09.800 --> 00:03:12.680 align:middle line:84%
viable or should you be
allowed to do it.

00:03:12.680 --> 00:03:15.792 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, so if maybe--

00:03:15.792 --> 00:03:18.072 align:middle line:84%
I'm trying to think how to
describe it-- so, people who

00:03:18.072 --> 00:03:21.310 align:middle line:84%
play casually will tend to say,
oh, if you do this with

00:03:21.310 --> 00:03:23.860 align:middle line:84%
this character then it's cheap
and you shouldn't do that,

00:03:23.860 --> 00:03:27.958 align:middle line:84%
whereas the community at large
and people who play seriously

00:03:27.958 --> 00:03:30.946 align:middle line:84%
tend to say, well, if you can
do something you should be

00:03:30.946 --> 00:03:34.183 align:middle line:84%
allowed to do it, and if
you can't overcome it

00:03:34.183 --> 00:03:36.080 align:middle line:90%
that's your own fault.

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:37.000 align:middle line:90%
GUEST SPEAKER: OK.

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:39.390 align:middle line:90%
So violating a community norm?

00:03:39.390 --> 00:03:40.550 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Yeah
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:03:40.550 --> 00:03:43.914 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: Even if it's
allowed in the rules but it's

00:03:43.914 --> 00:03:45.882 align:middle line:90%
against a community norm.

00:03:45.882 --> 00:03:47.132 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:03:47.132 --> 00:03:53.262 align:middle line:90%


00:03:53.262 --> 00:03:57.230 align:middle line:84%
It's like using
[UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:03:57.230 --> 00:03:59.720 align:middle line:90%
You hadn't thought about that.

00:03:59.720 --> 00:04:16.154 align:middle line:90%


00:04:16.154 --> 00:04:16.652 align:middle line:90%
Evan?

00:04:16.652 --> 00:04:17.648 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:04:17.648 --> 00:04:19.142 align:middle line:90%
GUEST SPEAKER: Oh, sorry.

00:04:19.142 --> 00:04:21.134 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I have a bunch of
stuff, which is related to

00:04:21.134 --> 00:04:24.640 align:middle line:90%
griefing just generally.

00:04:24.640 --> 00:04:30.180 align:middle line:84%
So let's just go for the one
which is one of my favorites.

00:04:30.180 --> 00:04:34.255 align:middle line:84%
So this is griefing for comic
effect, or role playing

00:04:34.255 --> 00:04:38.390 align:middle line:84%
griefing but doing it in a way
which you are then going to

00:04:38.390 --> 00:04:39.350 align:middle line:90%
write a blog about.

00:04:39.350 --> 00:04:42.510 align:middle line:84%
And from your character's
perspective the

00:04:42.510 --> 00:04:44.010 align:middle line:90%
griefing makes sense.

00:04:44.010 --> 00:04:47.770 align:middle line:84%
So there is a particular player
that played EverQuest.

00:04:47.770 --> 00:04:50.860 align:middle line:84%
His name is Fansy
the Famous Bard.

00:04:50.860 --> 00:04:58.150 align:middle line:84%
He was on the good team's side
and he griefed evil because

00:04:58.150 --> 00:05:00.210 align:middle line:90%
evil is bad.

00:05:00.210 --> 00:05:02.900 align:middle line:84%
So people who had played evil
characters, he would run

00:05:02.900 --> 00:05:05.730 align:middle line:84%
around and annoy in a really
frustrating way, which

00:05:05.730 --> 00:05:09.048 align:middle line:84%
eventually caused the makers
of EverQuest to change the

00:05:09.048 --> 00:05:12.025 align:middle line:84%
rules of the server so that
people could kill him, where

00:05:12.025 --> 00:05:13.755 align:middle line:84%
previously they weren't
allowed to.

00:05:13.755 --> 00:05:15.930 align:middle line:90%
GUEST SPEAKER: What did he do?

00:05:15.930 --> 00:05:19.790 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: There was this
mechanic in EverQuest where if

00:05:19.790 --> 00:05:22.834 align:middle line:84%
you were close to a monster
it became aggressive

00:05:22.834 --> 00:05:24.100 align:middle line:90%
and attacked you.

00:05:24.100 --> 00:05:27.220 align:middle line:84%
So he had a low level character
who was able to run

00:05:27.220 --> 00:05:28.280 align:middle line:90%
really fast and--

00:05:28.280 --> 00:05:29.000 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: I have
heard of him.

00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:30.720 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: --he would run around
and then run over the

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:34.976 align:middle line:84%
top of evil people, and then the
monsters would detach from

00:05:34.976 --> 00:05:38.554 align:middle line:90%
him and then kill them.

00:05:38.554 --> 00:05:44.360 align:middle line:84%
Oh yeah, and the catch
was that the server

00:05:44.360 --> 00:05:46.260 align:middle line:90%
was a mobile server.

00:05:46.260 --> 00:05:49.250 align:middle line:84%
Aside from-- that is why-- so
you can't complain to GMs

00:05:49.250 --> 00:05:53.970 align:middle line:84%
about a player's behavior,
aside from there was a

00:05:53.970 --> 00:05:57.890 align:middle line:84%
built-in protection for players
so that if they're

00:05:57.890 --> 00:06:01.460 align:middle line:84%
level four or below-- so a very
low level-- they can't be

00:06:01.460 --> 00:06:03.670 align:middle line:84%
killed by higher
level players.

00:06:03.670 --> 00:06:05.650 align:middle line:84%
And so he just sat on
the cusp of that.

00:06:05.650 --> 00:06:08.398 align:middle line:84%
Yet there's still a way to use
his ability to go around and

00:06:08.398 --> 00:06:09.780 align:middle line:84%
grief people from
a higher level.

00:06:09.780 --> 00:06:11.000 align:middle line:90%
It was pretty great.

00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:14.600 align:middle line:84%
And he did it all totally in
character as well, which is

00:06:14.600 --> 00:06:16.254 align:middle line:90%
what makes this a good grief.

00:06:16.254 --> 00:06:21.611 align:middle line:90%


00:06:21.611 --> 00:06:23.559 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Is that me?

00:06:23.559 --> 00:06:24.809 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:06:24.809 --> 00:06:26.968 align:middle line:90%


00:06:26.968 --> 00:06:31.838 align:middle line:84%
I think of Portal when they ask
you to use your portable

00:06:31.838 --> 00:06:32.325 align:middle line:90%
[UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:06:32.325 --> 00:06:34.145 align:middle line:90%
GUEST SPEAKER: [UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:06:34.145 --> 00:06:35.500 align:middle line:90%
You get to burn up the Cube.

00:06:35.500 --> 00:06:36.150 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:06:36.150 --> 00:06:37.955 align:middle line:84%
You don't want to, and
they make you.

00:06:37.955 --> 00:06:41.507 align:middle line:84%
And you've tried to leave
without it a few times and you

00:06:41.507 --> 00:06:43.735 align:middle line:84%
try to break the machine
and it doesn't work.

00:06:43.735 --> 00:06:45.220 align:middle line:90%
It's very sad.

00:06:45.220 --> 00:06:47.695 align:middle line:90%
GUEST SPEAKER: I feel bad.

00:06:47.695 --> 00:06:48.685 align:middle line:90%
[INAUDIBLE]

00:06:48.685 --> 00:06:48.808 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:06:48.808 --> 00:06:49.180 align:middle line:90%
How about you?

00:06:49.180 --> 00:06:53.434 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I thought of Jason
Rohrer's Sleep Is Death.

00:06:53.434 --> 00:06:55.382 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: What
is that like?

00:06:55.382 --> 00:06:57.750 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: It's a more of like a
story telling tool where you

00:06:57.750 --> 00:07:01.120 align:middle line:84%
have one player that can control
the 2D world and then

00:07:01.120 --> 00:07:03.859 align:middle line:84%
you have one player who is
the player, and they can

00:07:03.859 --> 00:07:05.104 align:middle line:90%
interact with it.

00:07:05.104 --> 00:07:08.390 align:middle line:84%
And each player has 30 seconds
to make some kind of action,

00:07:08.390 --> 00:07:10.450 align:middle line:84%
and then you make a story
going back and forth.

00:07:10.450 --> 00:07:13.020 align:middle line:90%
And what happens is--

00:07:13.020 --> 00:07:16.930 align:middle line:84%
you can play online with people,
and the controller

00:07:16.930 --> 00:07:19.800 align:middle line:84%
character or player will have
some kind of story that they

00:07:19.800 --> 00:07:20.250 align:middle line:90%
want to tell.

00:07:20.250 --> 00:07:23.290 align:middle line:84%
And so it's up to the player
whether or not they want to go

00:07:23.290 --> 00:07:25.690 align:middle line:84%
along with the story and act
like a character in that world

00:07:25.690 --> 00:07:28.360 align:middle line:90%
or try and break the story.

00:07:28.360 --> 00:07:29.300 align:middle line:90%
GUEST SPEAKER: OK.

00:07:29.300 --> 00:07:30.240 align:middle line:90%
What's the name of it again?

00:07:30.240 --> 00:07:31.150 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Sleep Is Death.

00:07:31.150 --> 00:07:34.470 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

00:07:34.470 --> 00:07:37.935 align:middle line:90%
I forgot what it's called.

00:07:37.935 --> 00:07:41.895 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: Actually that's
more griefing, would you say?

00:07:41.895 --> 00:07:43.380 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Kind of.

00:07:43.380 --> 00:07:46.350 align:middle line:90%


00:07:46.350 --> 00:07:48.330 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Is that by the same
person as [INAUDIBLE]

00:07:48.330 --> 00:07:48.825 align:middle line:90%
Passage?

00:07:48.825 --> 00:07:51.300 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yes.

00:07:51.300 --> 00:07:54.400 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: In Ocarina of Time
there's the treasure box

00:07:54.400 --> 00:07:55.110 align:middle line:90%
gambling game.

00:07:55.110 --> 00:07:57.830 align:middle line:84%
[UNINTELLIGIBLE] achieve if
you use the Lens of Truth.

00:07:57.830 --> 00:07:58.865 align:middle line:90%
[UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:07:58.865 --> 00:08:04.696 align:middle line:84%
So there's a built-in mechanic
that allows you to cheat.

00:08:04.696 --> 00:08:07.131 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: [UNINTELLIGIBLE]
legit.

00:08:07.131 --> 00:08:10.060 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:08:10.060 --> 00:08:14.540 align:middle line:84%
use it anyway so it's not
like a [UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:08:14.540 --> 00:08:16.410 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I just like include
the [UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:08:16.410 --> 00:08:19.030 align:middle line:90%


00:08:19.030 --> 00:08:20.990 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Lucky man.

00:08:20.990 --> 00:08:25.150 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So in Civ IV for each
and every sector there's

00:08:25.150 --> 00:08:29.130 align:middle line:84%
some sort of slavery mechanic,
but I guess in Civ IV's

00:08:29.130 --> 00:08:31.070 align:middle line:90%
example I know.

00:08:31.070 --> 00:08:35.350 align:middle line:84%
Many players don't seem to mind
the fact that what is

00:08:35.350 --> 00:08:38.649 align:middle line:84%
considered slavery is ethically
wrong, but in the

00:08:38.649 --> 00:08:43.200 align:middle line:84%
game it's affectionately called
whipping, and it's

00:08:43.200 --> 00:08:45.480 align:middle line:84%
considered a very efficient
way of converting food--

00:08:45.480 --> 00:08:45.840 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: What's
it called?

00:08:45.840 --> 00:08:46.730 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Whipping.

00:08:46.730 --> 00:08:48.710 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Whipping.

00:08:48.710 --> 00:08:51.185 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: You whip and then
you [INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:08:51.185 --> 00:08:55.145 align:middle line:90%


00:08:55.145 --> 00:08:56.135 align:middle line:90%
GUEST SPEAKER: OK.

00:08:56.135 --> 00:08:59.245 align:middle line:84%
So it's efficient but
it's slavery.

00:08:59.245 --> 00:09:02.330 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: You use granaries to
offset the population loss,

00:09:02.330 --> 00:09:03.700 align:middle line:90%
and if you [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:09:03.700 --> 00:09:09.000 align:middle line:84%
farms it becomes a very
effective way to produce,

00:09:09.000 --> 00:09:10.440 align:middle line:84%
especially in the
[UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:09:10.440 --> 00:09:12.757 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: [UNINTELLIGIBLE]
a lot of people that way.

00:09:12.757 --> 00:09:18.224 align:middle line:90%


00:09:18.224 --> 00:09:21.206 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So in The
Sims [INAUDIBLE].

00:09:21.206 --> 00:09:29.286 align:middle line:90%


00:09:29.286 --> 00:09:31.405 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: But you
have to watch them

00:09:31.405 --> 00:09:32.709 align:middle line:90%
flail around and die.

00:09:32.709 --> 00:09:35.690 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: And they get
tired and [INAUDIBLE]

00:09:35.690 --> 00:09:43.900 align:middle line:90%


00:09:43.900 --> 00:09:47.200 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: A more obscure example
that goes along with

00:09:47.200 --> 00:09:51.440 align:middle line:84%
[UNINTELLIGIBLE] griefing is
discussions over the point in

00:09:51.440 --> 00:09:54.510 align:middle line:84%
the game the community
is in MMOs.

00:09:54.510 --> 00:09:59.410 align:middle line:84%
Specifically, there was
apparently a huge problem in

00:09:59.410 --> 00:10:03.550 align:middle line:84%
an obscure MMO called Skyress
where a large community of the

