00:19:40.280 --> 00:19:42.470 align:middle line:84%
days but I couldn't play
before 2:00 PM.

00:19:42.470 --> 00:19:44.676 align:middle line:90%
So I come in at 2:00 PM.

00:19:44.676 --> 00:19:48.556 align:middle line:84%
How many items was I allowed to
draw when I came into the

00:19:48.556 --> 00:19:50.630 align:middle line:84%
game given that I already missed
two hours of the game?

00:19:50.630 --> 00:19:55.190 align:middle line:84%
And if the answer is, well, I
can make that up, then what

00:19:55.190 --> 00:19:57.422 align:middle line:84%
happens if I miss an hour
half way through the--

00:19:57.422 --> 00:20:01.330 align:middle line:90%
and if I forget.

00:20:01.330 --> 00:20:03.820 align:middle line:90%
No one's looking into the box.

00:20:03.820 --> 00:20:04.950 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: What
did you do?

00:20:04.950 --> 00:20:05.923 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: What did I do?

00:20:05.923 --> 00:20:06.650 align:middle line:90%
GUEST SPEAKER: Yes.

00:20:06.650 --> 00:20:07.376 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: What did I do?

00:20:07.376 --> 00:20:10.285 align:middle line:84%
I think I drew more than one
item at the beginning of the

00:20:10.285 --> 00:20:14.100 align:middle line:84%
game and I made sure
I never forgot.

00:20:14.100 --> 00:20:15.435 align:middle line:90%
So, honor system.

00:20:15.435 --> 00:20:17.856 align:middle line:84%
A lot of LARPs are based
on the honor system.

00:20:17.856 --> 00:20:23.832 align:middle line:90%


00:20:23.832 --> 00:20:27.990 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So in Final Fantasy
VII, like a lot of Japanese

00:20:27.990 --> 00:20:30.060 align:middle line:84%
RPGs, you get used to the idea
of walking into people's

00:20:30.060 --> 00:20:33.750 align:middle line:84%
houses and just digging in the
items you find, except in FF

00:20:33.750 --> 00:20:35.620 align:middle line:84%
VII they actually mix it
up a couple of times.

00:20:35.620 --> 00:20:37.440 align:middle line:84%
There are a couple of instances
where you go in

00:20:37.440 --> 00:20:39.570 align:middle line:84%
there and do that and maybe it's
fairly insignificant, you

00:20:39.570 --> 00:20:42.800 align:middle line:84%
could buy it easily with a
couple pieces of gold or gil

00:20:42.800 --> 00:20:43.915 align:middle line:90%
or whatever it is.

00:20:43.915 --> 00:20:45.940 align:middle line:84%
And then the person walks out of
their kitchen or something,

00:20:45.940 --> 00:20:47.630 align:middle line:84%
and it's, did you just
take my item?

00:20:47.630 --> 00:20:50.070 align:middle line:84%
And you have the option
saying yes or no.

00:20:50.070 --> 00:20:53.070 align:middle line:84%
And if you say no then you get
to keep it but if you say yes

00:20:53.070 --> 00:20:54.150 align:middle line:90%
they're, what the hell?

00:20:54.150 --> 00:20:57.482 align:middle line:90%
Give it back.

00:20:57.482 --> 00:21:00.891 align:middle line:90%
I thought it was interesting.

00:21:00.891 --> 00:21:02.717 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I have something
that hasn't really been

00:21:02.717 --> 00:21:05.790 align:middle line:90%
touched on.

00:21:05.790 --> 00:21:09.721 align:middle line:84%
When you're running a very large
scale Guild in an MMO

00:21:09.721 --> 00:21:12.777 align:middle line:84%
you reach a point where you need
to distribute from these

00:21:12.777 --> 00:21:20.320 align:middle line:84%
high end The community optimal
thing to do is for the people

00:21:20.320 --> 00:21:23.565 align:middle line:84%
who are in charge of the Guild
to allocate who gets what so

00:21:23.565 --> 00:21:26.305 align:middle line:84%
that you can, for instance, kick
out the characters that's

00:21:26.305 --> 00:21:27.910 align:middle line:84%
most important for the Guild
to have for the [? game ?]

00:21:27.910 --> 00:21:29.760 align:middle line:90%
to progress.

00:21:29.760 --> 00:21:33.040 align:middle line:84%
But for the most part, actual
members of the Guild don't

00:21:33.040 --> 00:21:36.380 align:middle line:84%
really like that because you
would like to receive some

00:21:36.380 --> 00:21:39.235 align:middle line:84%
kind of reward for having put
in time to the Guild.

00:21:39.235 --> 00:21:42.445 align:middle line:84%
And going a long time without
ever having gotten anything is

00:21:42.445 --> 00:21:46.130 align:middle line:84%
really frustrating, so people
come up with these intricate

00:21:46.130 --> 00:21:52.920 align:middle line:84%
systems for DKP for distributing
your resources,

00:21:52.920 --> 00:21:56.564 align:middle line:84%
essentially, to try to balance
the interests of the

00:21:56.564 --> 00:21:58.387 align:middle line:84%
individual and the interest
of the community.

00:21:58.387 --> 00:22:02.110 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: How many of you
are unsure what DKP is?

00:22:02.110 --> 00:22:04.576 align:middle line:90%
Dragon Kill Points.

00:22:04.576 --> 00:22:08.110 align:middle line:84%
There are these sometimes
elaborate schemes for if you

00:22:08.110 --> 00:22:13.190 align:middle line:84%
participate in these raids,
you get so many points.

00:22:13.190 --> 00:22:18.050 align:middle line:84%
And the idea is the Guild claims
everything that drops

00:22:18.050 --> 00:22:18.980 align:middle line:90%
physically.

00:22:18.980 --> 00:22:22.555 align:middle line:84%
And you build up points, and at
some point you have enough

00:22:22.555 --> 00:22:25.020 align:middle line:90%
to buy a certain item.

00:22:25.020 --> 00:22:27.440 align:middle line:84%
So let's say you're on a raid
and you just got something

00:22:27.440 --> 00:22:28.860 align:middle line:90%
really cool last time.

00:22:28.860 --> 00:22:31.130 align:middle line:84%
If something drops this time
that you could really use,

00:22:31.130 --> 00:22:33.580 align:middle line:84%
even though you're there you
don't necessarily get it

00:22:33.580 --> 00:22:36.920 align:middle line:84%
because you just spent
all your DKP.

00:22:36.920 --> 00:22:40.670 align:middle line:84%
And there's lots of Guild drama
over how these DKPs--

00:22:40.670 --> 00:22:42.926 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Primary source
of Guild drama.

00:22:42.926 --> 00:22:44.335 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Have you
been playing?

00:22:44.335 --> 00:22:45.185 align:middle line:90%
GUEST SPEAKER: Hm?

00:22:45.185 --> 00:22:48.240 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Dan, have you
been playing this?

00:22:48.240 --> 00:22:50.026 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: You end up by
raids and then you

00:22:50.026 --> 00:22:51.840 align:middle line:90%
catch them before breakfast.

00:22:51.840 --> 00:22:54.616 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: And there
are black out dates.

00:22:54.616 --> 00:22:56.380 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Running one of these
things is actually

00:22:56.380 --> 00:23:00.120 align:middle line:84%
surprisingly complicated and
hard, particularly--

00:23:00.120 --> 00:23:03.880 align:middle line:84%
so my little experience,
I ran a Guild which

00:23:03.880 --> 00:23:06.100 align:middle line:90%
incorporated this.

00:23:06.100 --> 00:23:08.710 align:middle line:84%
One problem that happens is if
you have fixed prices for

00:23:08.710 --> 00:23:13.370 align:middle line:84%
things then you get DKP
inflation as prices change.

00:23:13.370 --> 00:23:15.990 align:middle line:84%
And what happens is-- and it's
incredibly complicated-- to

00:23:15.990 --> 00:23:17.640 align:middle line:84%
where, now, you have to reprice
everything constantly.

00:23:17.640 --> 00:23:20.180 align:middle line:84%
So instead we went for an
auction-based system where

00:23:20.180 --> 00:23:22.840 align:middle line:90%
people bid on things.

00:23:22.840 --> 00:23:25.470 align:middle line:84%
And then prices correct
themselves.

00:23:25.470 --> 00:23:29.360 align:middle line:84%
However, people can collude in
auctions, which then causes a

00:23:29.360 --> 00:23:31.775 align:middle line:84%
resentment in people who aren't
in on the collusion,

00:23:31.775 --> 00:23:34.970 align:middle line:84%
and the whole thing is
just a nightmare.

00:23:34.970 --> 00:23:36.890 align:middle line:84%
And now I don't run a Guild
anymore and I'm really happy.

00:23:36.890 --> 00:23:41.183 align:middle line:90%


00:23:41.183 --> 00:23:44.610 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: In games like Pokemon
or Animal Crossing you

00:23:44.610 --> 00:23:47.180 align:middle line:84%
can decide whether or not
to change the time on

00:23:47.180 --> 00:23:50.070 align:middle line:90%
your DNS or game cube.

00:23:50.070 --> 00:23:52.270 align:middle line:84%
[UNINTELLIGIBLE] score
came out I was like

00:23:52.270 --> 00:23:54.509 align:middle line:90%
10 I had a bed time.

00:23:54.509 --> 00:23:57.407 align:middle line:84%
I didn't have a lot of time
to catch up on Pokemon.

00:23:57.407 --> 00:24:01.760 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: You can do that too
and manipulate the RNG and get

00:24:01.760 --> 00:24:03.400 align:middle line:90%
the stats you want.

00:24:03.400 --> 00:24:04.760 align:middle line:84%
Perfect for
[INTERPOSING VOICES].

00:24:04.760 --> 00:24:08.144 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: There's also-- besides
whenever you do the

00:24:08.144 --> 00:24:13.520 align:middle line:84%
random number generator hacks
in games in order to get a

00:24:13.520 --> 00:24:17.160 align:middle line:84%
certain drop that's normally 1%
but you can increase it to

00:24:17.160 --> 00:24:20.340 align:middle line:90%
up to almost 100--

00:24:20.340 --> 00:24:24.910 align:middle line:84%
but what I was going to say was
that in Fallout 3 you can

00:24:24.910 --> 00:24:26.750 align:middle line:90%
pickpocket people.

00:24:26.750 --> 00:24:29.242 align:middle line:84%
And something you do when you
pickpocket people, as long as

00:24:29.242 --> 00:24:32.840 align:middle line:84%
you don't get caught, you can
take a grenade from your

00:24:32.840 --> 00:24:35.270 align:middle line:84%
inventory and place it in their
pocket, and all of a

00:24:35.270 --> 00:24:37.870 align:middle line:84%
sudden they're, oh my god,
there's a grenade, run away.

00:24:37.870 --> 00:24:39.360 align:middle line:84%
And it will blow up inside
their pocket

00:24:39.360 --> 00:24:40.722 align:middle line:90%
and they will explode.

00:24:40.722 --> 00:24:41.720 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: What game?

00:24:41.720 --> 00:24:43.160 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Fallout 3.

00:24:43.160 --> 00:24:47.130 align:middle line:84%
And the funny thing is that
that's actually an achievement

00:24:47.130 --> 00:24:50.088 align:middle line:90%
to blow someone up.

00:24:50.088 --> 00:24:51.980 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: Do
you get, other

00:24:51.980 --> 00:24:53.750 align:middle line:90%
than the joy and giggles--

00:24:53.750 --> 00:24:57.260 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Well besides from
the joy and giggles,

00:24:57.260 --> 00:24:59.430 align:middle line:90%
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:24:59.430 --> 00:25:02.280 align:middle line:84%
you can pick their pockets,
whatever they have in their

00:25:02.280 --> 00:25:03.110 align:middle line:90%
pocket you can get.

00:25:03.110 --> 00:25:04.870 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: That is still left.

00:25:04.870 --> 00:25:07.521 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Well you take
it out as you're

00:25:07.521 --> 00:25:08.977 align:middle line:90%
putting the grenade in.

00:25:08.977 --> 00:25:11.033 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: You wouldn't want
their [UNINTELLIGIBLE] to be

00:25:11.033 --> 00:25:11.719 align:middle line:90%
damaged by the grenade.

00:25:11.719 --> 00:25:13.830 align:middle line:90%
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:25:13.830 --> 00:25:14.937 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Yeah, you're
just putting them

00:25:14.937 --> 00:25:16.260 align:middle line:90%
in at the same time.

00:25:16.260 --> 00:25:18.125 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:25:18.125 --> 00:25:20.422 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: And it can
be a little tricky.

00:25:20.422 --> 00:25:21.672 align:middle line:90%
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:25:21.672 --> 00:25:25.878 align:middle line:90%


00:25:25.878 --> 00:25:26.870 align:middle line:90%
GUEST SPEAKER: OK.

00:25:26.870 --> 00:25:28.840 align:middle line:84%
Anybody have any representative
examples we

00:25:28.840 --> 00:25:30.286 align:middle line:90%
haven't touched on?

00:25:30.286 --> 00:25:33.258 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I used to play a lot
of [UNINTELLIGIBLE] online and

00:25:33.258 --> 00:25:34.624 align:middle line:84%
they had a bunch of really
sophisticated rules that

00:25:34.624 --> 00:25:37.040 align:middle line:90%
applied in certain situations.

00:25:37.040 --> 00:25:40.280 align:middle line:84%
So there were a lot of times
in the game where you just

00:25:40.280 --> 00:25:43.172 align:middle line:84%
flat out lie to people and have
them go into situations

00:25:43.172 --> 00:25:45.000 align:middle line:90%
that were unsafe for them.

00:25:45.000 --> 00:25:47.950 align:middle line:84%
For example, if people were in
your house-- in an urban

00:25:47.950 --> 00:25:49.750 align:middle line:84%
[UNINTELLIGIBLE] game if people
are inside your house

00:25:49.750 --> 00:25:53.390 align:middle line:84%
they are automatically flagged
as attackable by you because

00:25:53.390 --> 00:25:56.155 align:middle line:84%
they're in your house and you
might not have let them in.

00:25:56.155 --> 00:25:57.990 align:middle line:84%
And they can run outside their
house and they'll be

00:25:57.990 --> 00:25:58.730 align:middle line:90%
unattackable by you.

00:25:58.730 --> 00:26:01.930 align:middle line:84%
But you can also put a trash
barrel in front of your door,

00:26:01.930 --> 00:26:04.480 align:middle line:84%
which only you can destroy and
nobody else can destroy, and

00:26:04.480 --> 00:26:05.910 align:middle line:90%
they can't run through that.

00:26:05.910 --> 00:26:08.570 align:middle line:84%
So you would invite them in
to trade them something or

00:26:08.570 --> 00:26:09.395 align:middle line:90%
enjoying your meal.

00:26:09.395 --> 00:26:11.830 align:middle line:84%
And they're in your house, then
you put a trash barrel

00:26:11.830 --> 00:26:16.000 align:middle line:84%
there so they can never leave
and just kill them.

00:26:16.000 --> 00:26:16.740 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: Or just leave
them there, right?

00:26:16.740 --> 00:26:17.710 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Or just leave them.

00:26:17.710 --> 00:26:19.640 align:middle line:84%
There are tons and
tons of rules.

00:26:19.640 --> 00:26:21.490 align:middle line:90%
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:26:21.490 --> 00:26:23.645 align:middle line:84%
It's really bad, It's
really bad.

00:26:23.645 --> 00:26:27.889 align:middle line:84%
I was part of the Guild that
did this to me and then I

00:26:27.889 --> 00:26:28.887 align:middle line:90%
[UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:26:28.887 --> 00:26:30.883 align:middle line:84%
I was like, that
is pretty sick.

00:26:30.883 --> 00:26:33.378 align:middle line:84%
There are a bunch of way to
steal tons and tons of money

00:26:33.378 --> 00:26:35.873 align:middle line:84%
from people and they didn't
realize that you did.

00:26:35.873 --> 00:26:37.370 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: Did you get
that [UNINTELLIGIBLE]?

00:26:37.370 --> 00:26:38.368 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:26:38.368 --> 00:26:38.867 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:26:38.867 --> 00:26:43.460 align:middle line:84%
But I feel like just blatantly
lying to people as a tactic

00:26:43.460 --> 00:26:45.310 align:middle line:84%
instead of just being
[UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:26:45.310 --> 00:26:48.150 align:middle line:90%


00:26:48.150 --> 00:26:51.713 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: The question about
real world money for in game

00:26:51.713 --> 00:26:56.555 align:middle line:84%
items especially for competitive
games whether or

00:26:56.555 --> 00:26:58.501 align:middle line:90%
not it's allowed by the GMs.

00:26:58.501 --> 00:27:02.140 align:middle line:90%


00:27:02.140 --> 00:27:03.556 align:middle line:84%
Those are two different
questions.

00:27:03.556 --> 00:27:05.640 align:middle line:84%
One is the ethical question from
the player's standpoint.

00:27:05.640 --> 00:27:09.119 align:middle line:84%
And the other is what does it
mean in terms of the player

00:27:09.119 --> 00:27:12.430 align:middle line:84%
character relation
in the game.

00:27:12.430 --> 00:27:16.230 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I was going to say
one thing that comes up

00:27:16.230 --> 00:27:19.900 align:middle line:84%
especially in board games that
have a lot of positive

00:27:19.900 --> 00:27:23.030 align:middle line:84%
feedback, it's called
king making.

00:27:23.030 --> 00:27:24.315 align:middle line:84%
Good examples are like
in Monopoly,

00:27:24.315 --> 00:27:25.530 align:middle line:90%
or Settlers or Munchkin.

00:27:25.530 --> 00:27:29.375 align:middle line:84%
And how it works, let's say you
and Owen and I are playing

00:27:29.375 --> 00:27:33.000 align:middle line:84%
Settlers and typically what
will happen is say Owen is

00:27:33.000 --> 00:27:35.750 align:middle line:84%
winning but I'm typically really
far behind, or someone

00:27:35.750 --> 00:27:38.250 align:middle line:84%
is going to be in a position
where they have

00:27:38.250 --> 00:27:39.540 align:middle line:90%
no chance of winning.

00:27:39.540 --> 00:27:42.123 align:middle line:84%
So if I decide he's winning and
you're close second and

00:27:42.123 --> 00:27:45.271 align:middle line:84%
I'm distant third, what people
do is say, hey Mia, I will

00:27:45.271 --> 00:27:47.640 align:middle line:84%
give you everything for your one
little card and then you

00:27:47.640 --> 00:27:50.390 align:middle line:84%
can win because I'm
mad at Owen.

00:27:50.390 --> 00:27:52.844 align:middle line:84%
And then it comes up, like you
said, in Monopoly there's this

00:27:52.844 --> 00:27:55.356 align:middle line:84%
question of is that a really
jerky thing to do?

00:27:55.356 --> 00:27:56.832 align:middle line:90%
The rules don't disallow it.

00:27:56.832 --> 00:27:59.292 align:middle line:90%
It's an ongoing debate.

00:27:59.292 --> 00:28:01.752 align:middle line:90%
[UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:28:01.752 --> 00:28:03.720 align:middle line:90%
GUEST SPEAKER: Don't do that.

00:28:03.720 --> 00:28:06.672 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Similarly in Monopoly,
the rules don't

00:28:06.672 --> 00:28:08.640 align:middle line:84%
specifically allow If I have
two [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:28:08.640 --> 00:28:12.820 align:middle line:84%
we'll exchange that and promise
each other immunity on

00:28:12.820 --> 00:28:14.940 align:middle line:84%
our properties or something
that you're not allowed to

00:28:14.940 --> 00:28:15.730 align:middle line:90%
actually promise each other.

00:28:15.730 --> 00:28:17.760 align:middle line:90%
Or if [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:28:17.760 --> 00:28:20.632 align:middle line:84%
will always do it to you and
will promise me half the

00:28:20.632 --> 00:28:23.246 align:middle line:84%
profits or something like that
where clearly the third person

00:28:23.246 --> 00:28:26.418 align:middle line:84%
is now shut out of the game
and can't win because

00:28:26.418 --> 00:28:27.412 align:middle line:90%
[UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:28:27.412 --> 00:28:31.388 align:middle line:84%
advantage even though we're
[UNINTELLIGIBLE] with things

00:28:31.388 --> 00:28:33.376 align:middle line:84%
that aren't explicitly
allowed in the rules.

00:28:33.376 --> 00:28:37.120 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: How many of you
have played Dragon Age?

00:28:37.120 --> 00:28:39.595 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: I've seen it played.

00:28:39.595 --> 00:28:40.585 align:middle line:90%
GUEST SPEAKER: OK.

00:28:40.585 --> 00:28:43.555 align:middle line:84%
I've just, I'm playing
through it right now.

00:28:43.555 --> 00:28:44.720 align:middle line:84%
I've been thinking about
these issues a lot.

00:28:44.720 --> 00:28:47.069 align:middle line:84%
Actually on my first play
through there's a scene where

00:28:47.069 --> 00:28:49.021 align:middle line:84%
there's a kid possessed
by a demon.

00:28:49.021 --> 00:28:50.100 align:middle line:84%
You have the choice whether
you kill him or not.

00:28:50.100 --> 00:28:53.734 align:middle line:84%
The first time I killed
him and--

00:28:53.734 --> 00:28:56.560 align:middle line:90%
I thought everybody did.

00:28:56.560 --> 00:28:58.270 align:middle line:90%
I had no compunction about it.

