AUDIENCE: It isn't telling
you which choice

00:38:03.056 --> 00:38:03.304 align:middle line:90%
is the right option.

00:38:03.304 --> 00:38:07.870 align:middle line:84%
You needn't be morally
[UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:38:07.870 --> 00:38:09.540 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: What
do you think?

00:38:09.540 --> 00:38:11.830 align:middle line:90%
Do you think he's saying--

00:38:11.830 --> 00:38:13.205 align:middle line:84%
I know what you're saying
because he also mentioned Ice

00:38:13.205 --> 00:38:15.315 align:middle line:84%
Field Republic as
a closed option.

00:38:15.315 --> 00:38:18.330 align:middle line:90%


00:38:18.330 --> 00:38:22.870 align:middle line:84%
Is it that there are two
discrete, like good or evil,

00:38:22.870 --> 00:38:26.700 align:middle line:84%
like the Jedi or
the dark path?

00:38:26.700 --> 00:38:29.400 align:middle line:90%
Is Fallout that concrete?

00:38:29.400 --> 00:38:31.050 align:middle line:90%
Fallout 3.

00:38:31.050 --> 00:38:34.950 align:middle line:90%
I know inFAMOUS is.

00:38:34.950 --> 00:38:37.760 align:middle line:84%
You're either going one
way or the other.

00:38:37.760 --> 00:38:40.025 align:middle line:84%
But I'm not as sure, and I'm
just asking people who've

00:38:40.025 --> 00:38:44.606 align:middle line:84%
played it, is Fallout 3 that
particular in is it setting up

00:38:44.606 --> 00:38:45.928 align:middle line:84%
a dichotomy of the good
character versus

00:38:45.928 --> 00:38:48.373 align:middle line:90%
evil, or is it much--

00:38:48.373 --> 00:38:49.840 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: It's much more
of a gradient.

00:38:49.840 --> 00:38:51.796 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:38:51.796 --> 00:38:53.752 align:middle line:90%
[UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:38:53.752 --> 00:38:56.020 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: In Baldur's Gate
there's two things.

00:38:56.020 --> 00:38:57.310 align:middle line:84%
There's your alignment,
which you pick at the

00:38:57.310 --> 00:38:58.998 align:middle line:90%
beginning of the game.

00:38:58.998 --> 00:39:02.832 align:middle line:84%
It's more like D&D style
but it's fixed.

00:39:02.832 --> 00:39:07.970 align:middle line:84%
But then there's this thing
that's your reputation, which

00:39:07.970 --> 00:39:11.400 align:middle line:84%
can be decreased if you do
certain things, from the game

00:39:11.400 --> 00:39:13.545 align:middle line:90%
point of view, in view others.

00:39:13.545 --> 00:39:16.530 align:middle line:84%
So you can get into doing bad
stuff sometimes without

00:39:16.530 --> 00:39:18.170 align:middle line:84%
actually decreasing
your reputation.

00:39:18.170 --> 00:39:22.802 align:middle line:84%
You can also just pick a evil
alignment and then do only

00:39:22.802 --> 00:39:25.142 align:middle line:84%
good things, and that's
just very weird.

00:39:25.142 --> 00:39:27.860 align:middle line:84%
So I don't exactly remember
how the alignment really

00:39:27.860 --> 00:39:29.020 align:middle line:90%
factors into game play.

00:39:29.020 --> 00:39:30.370 align:middle line:84%
But there's a moment, for
example, in the game, there's

00:39:30.370 --> 00:39:31.970 align:middle line:90%
a big story for--

00:39:31.970 --> 00:39:35.460 align:middle line:84%
it rejoins it afterwards-- but
you you have to ally yourself

00:39:35.460 --> 00:39:39.960 align:middle line:84%
with either the Thieves Guild
or the vampires in town who

00:39:39.960 --> 00:39:42.420 align:middle line:90%
are competing affections.

00:39:42.420 --> 00:39:44.700 align:middle line:84%
So you have the thieves
against the vampires.

00:39:44.700 --> 00:39:46.726 align:middle line:84%
If it's the thieves against the
vampires you got to pick

00:39:46.726 --> 00:39:52.790 align:middle line:84%
one to help you raid a certain
area, and you don't really

00:39:52.790 --> 00:39:54.160 align:middle line:90%
have other choices.

00:39:54.160 --> 00:39:58.020 align:middle line:84%
And one doesn't make you
gooder or eviler.

00:39:58.020 --> 00:39:59.070 align:middle line:90%
They're both kind of bad.

00:39:59.070 --> 00:40:01.800 align:middle line:84%
But you have to do it to
find the person that

00:40:01.800 --> 00:40:03.740 align:middle line:90%
you're trying to get.

00:40:03.740 --> 00:40:06.650 align:middle line:90%
That's kind of closed.

00:40:06.650 --> 00:40:10.200 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So in terms of
Baldur's Gate II, I would say,

00:40:10.200 --> 00:40:12.790 align:middle line:84%
one, there's the reputation
stats, which I've found never

00:40:12.790 --> 00:40:14.310 align:middle line:84%
really into the
[UNINTELLIGIBLE], which is

00:40:14.310 --> 00:40:15.700 align:middle line:90%
some type of metric for your--

00:40:15.700 --> 00:40:17.290 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: That's not true.

00:40:17.290 --> 00:40:19.696 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: You kill everyone
and you have to go

00:40:19.696 --> 00:40:21.690 align:middle line:90%
[UNINTELLIGIBLE] to do that.

00:40:21.690 --> 00:40:24.300 align:middle line:84%
People in your party can duel
you for your honor if they get

00:40:24.300 --> 00:40:25.280 align:middle line:90%
upset at you.

00:40:25.280 --> 00:40:28.260 align:middle line:84%
Kaldor dueled me, left my
party, and then tried to

00:40:28.260 --> 00:40:30.710 align:middle line:84%
attack me because
I was too evil.

00:40:30.710 --> 00:40:31.690 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:40:31.690 --> 00:40:32.460 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:40:32.460 --> 00:40:32.990 align:middle line:90%
I stood out.

00:40:32.990 --> 00:40:35.440 align:middle line:84%
Like very, very specific actions
that will affect your

00:40:35.440 --> 00:40:37.795 align:middle line:84%
alignment, and there aren't
very many of those.

00:40:37.795 --> 00:40:41.310 align:middle line:84%
In addition you have these cut
scene decisions, which are

00:40:41.310 --> 00:40:43.326 align:middle line:84%
also very black or white,
good or evil.

00:40:43.326 --> 00:40:45.522 align:middle line:84%
But I would say there's a lot of
decisions in the game that

00:40:45.522 --> 00:40:49.470 align:middle line:84%
don't [UNINTELLIGIBLE], that
have both that ethical choice

00:40:49.470 --> 00:40:54.980 align:middle line:84%
but not nearly as much of
a mechanical impact.

00:40:54.980 --> 00:40:57.010 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: One of the things
he also mentions in

00:40:57.010 --> 00:41:00.508 align:middle line:84%
relation to the open system,
going back to The Sims, is

00:41:00.508 --> 00:41:05.470 align:middle line:84%
that you can do what you want
and the game doesn't

00:41:05.470 --> 00:41:08.040 align:middle line:90%
necessarily punish you.

00:41:08.040 --> 00:41:11.480 align:middle line:84%
You can murder and you can drown
your Sims, and the rest

00:41:11.480 --> 00:41:15.290 align:middle line:84%
of the Sims in the house don't
move away in fear or

00:41:15.290 --> 00:41:18.010 align:middle line:90%
disapprove of your action.

00:41:18.010 --> 00:41:20.270 align:middle line:84%
It's just up to you whether
you want to feel

00:41:20.270 --> 00:41:21.840 align:middle line:90%
bad about it or not.

00:41:21.840 --> 00:41:27.990 align:middle line:84%
Or the game has no larger
response other than let a Sim

00:41:27.990 --> 00:41:31.745 align:middle line:84%
die once the site lit
a Sim on fire.

00:41:31.745 --> 00:41:36.200 align:middle line:84%
And I wish they rolled for his
life and got a zombie back.

00:41:36.200 --> 00:41:39.830 align:middle line:84%
It turns out the only way
zombies happen in the game is

00:41:39.830 --> 00:41:40.655 align:middle line:84%
if you make somebody
else unhappy.

00:41:40.655 --> 00:41:44.120 align:middle line:84%
So then I had to
kill a zombie.

00:41:44.120 --> 00:41:45.120 align:middle line:90%
It was sad.

00:41:45.120 --> 00:41:48.070 align:middle line:84%
So that was another
element to it.

00:41:48.070 --> 00:41:49.900 align:middle line:84%
One of the things that he talks
about in general in

00:41:49.900 --> 00:41:55.030 align:middle line:84%
relation to all of this, and a
lot of these fit is ethics to

00:41:55.030 --> 00:41:57.570 align:middle line:90%
your statistics.

00:41:57.570 --> 00:42:01.190 align:middle line:84%
That somehow ethical systems are
on this point or sliding

00:42:01.190 --> 00:42:03.685 align:middle line:84%
scale and that you're heading
towards good or you're heading

00:42:03.685 --> 00:42:04.560 align:middle line:90%
towards evil.

00:42:04.560 --> 00:42:07.880 align:middle line:84%
We were even mentioning this
before class started that you

00:42:07.880 --> 00:42:11.530 align:middle line:84%
can blow up an entire town but
then if you give three beggars

00:42:11.530 --> 00:42:15.215 align:middle line:84%
$0.50 cents each suddenly you're
back at neutral because

00:42:15.215 --> 00:42:18.804 align:middle line:84%
of the way that these sliding
systems work in bizzarely

00:42:18.804 --> 00:42:20.090 align:middle line:90%
mechanical ways.

00:42:20.090 --> 00:42:22.144 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Yeah, you can
buy reputation back

00:42:22.144 --> 00:42:24.092 align:middle line:90%
by stoning two temples.

00:42:24.092 --> 00:42:26.040 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: It's like
indulgences.

00:42:26.040 --> 00:42:27.680 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah it is, exactly.

00:42:27.680 --> 00:42:29.120 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Indulgences
[INAUDIBLE].

00:42:29.120 --> 00:42:32.480 align:middle line:90%


00:42:32.480 --> 00:42:35.360 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I have to close this
now because I'm getting high

00:42:35.360 --> 00:42:36.320 align:middle line:90%
off of marker fumes.

00:42:36.320 --> 00:42:40.064 align:middle line:84%
