shoot at enemies who are coming
onto your center but at

00:47:32.210 --> 00:47:36.130 align:middle line:84%
such a range that you cannot
tell friend from foe.

00:47:36.130 --> 00:47:39.030 align:middle line:84%
That it's absolutely certain
that whatever it is you can

00:47:39.030 --> 00:47:41.680 align:middle line:84%
get there's a 50% chance
that there's

00:47:41.680 --> 00:47:43.840 align:middle line:90%
actually one of your allies.

00:47:43.840 --> 00:47:45.950 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: There's also
the really creepy--

00:47:45.950 --> 00:47:47.050 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: Gunship sequence.

00:47:47.050 --> 00:47:49.025 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: --the gunship
sequence, which is--

00:47:49.025 --> 00:47:50.750 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: Which gets most
of the attention.

00:47:50.750 --> 00:47:59.146 align:middle line:84%
But I was surprised by the
subtlety of the sequence where

00:47:59.146 --> 00:48:02.279 align:middle line:84%
you are just in the middle of a
firefight in Modern Warfare

00:48:02.279 --> 00:48:06.230 align:middle line:84%
1 because it has that situation
of you are being

00:48:06.230 --> 00:48:13.445 align:middle line:84%
told to kill but you may not,
even though everyone that you

00:48:13.445 --> 00:48:16.090 align:middle line:84%
might be shooting at is a
combatant, you may not want to

00:48:16.090 --> 00:48:19.140 align:middle line:90%
shoot everyone in this space.

00:48:19.140 --> 00:48:21.720 align:middle line:84%
I felt that made a more
interesting, subtle point

00:48:21.720 --> 00:48:22.150 align:middle line:90%
[UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:48:22.150 --> 00:48:26.180 align:middle line:84%
That's kind of what the
situation of being in the

00:48:26.180 --> 00:48:31.264 align:middle line:84%
Middle East right now is like,
as far as you can tell.

00:48:31.264 --> 00:48:34.570 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: I was thinking
about this because I actually

00:48:34.570 --> 00:48:39.260 align:middle line:84%
did an interview last week, two
weeks ago, with some radio

00:48:39.260 --> 00:48:42.150 align:middle line:84%
people and it was about Black
Ops being released because

00:48:42.150 --> 00:48:45.190 align:middle line:84%
it's the best selling
entertainment media ever.

00:48:45.190 --> 00:48:47.750 align:middle line:84%
And they introduced it, of
course, on the segment as the

00:48:47.750 --> 00:48:51.620 align:middle line:84%
ultra violent video game,
Call of Duty.

00:48:51.620 --> 00:48:53.560 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: That's pretty
accurate.

00:48:53.560 --> 00:48:55.970 align:middle line:90%
GUEST SPEAKER: It is please.

00:48:55.970 --> 00:49:00.370 align:middle line:84%
And one of the questions that
each affiliate asked over and

00:49:00.370 --> 00:49:03.850 align:middle line:84%
over is they mentioned the
Castro level and they said,

00:49:03.850 --> 00:49:07.290 align:middle line:90%
have video games gone too far?

00:49:07.290 --> 00:49:10.830 align:middle line:84%
Is there a line that shouldn't
be crossed?

00:49:10.830 --> 00:49:14.730 align:middle line:84%
And I'm sure if they had-- none
of these people actually

00:49:14.730 --> 00:49:17.285 align:middle line:84%
played video games
so they were just

00:49:17.285 --> 00:49:19.250 align:middle line:90%
reading their scripts--

00:49:19.250 --> 00:49:22.400 align:middle line:84%
this might have even fit
even better in terms of

00:49:22.400 --> 00:49:23.950 align:middle line:90%
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:49:23.950 --> 00:49:27.136 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I'm surprised
this got through.

00:49:27.136 --> 00:49:29.750 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: Well it's
an optional level.

00:49:29.750 --> 00:49:31.830 align:middle line:90%
You don't have to do it.

00:49:31.830 --> 00:49:33.560 align:middle line:84%
Interesting comment
right there.

00:49:33.560 --> 00:49:36.800 align:middle line:84%
If it was JFK airport
or US citizens--

00:49:36.800 --> 00:49:42.440 align:middle line:84%
But leaving aside just the knee
jerk gamer defense, well,

00:49:42.440 --> 00:49:45.690 align:middle line:84%
it's just for fun or it's just
a video game, how do you

00:49:45.690 --> 00:49:47.250 align:middle line:90%
answer that question?

00:49:47.250 --> 00:49:51.580 align:middle line:84%
I know what I spew to them but
I'm just curious in terms of

00:49:51.580 --> 00:49:56.150 align:middle line:84%
what does it mean beyond just
games as statistics for the

00:49:56.150 --> 00:49:57.660 align:middle line:90%
good or the dark side?

00:49:57.660 --> 00:50:00.460 align:middle line:90%


00:50:00.460 --> 00:50:02.330 align:middle line:84%
Is there a point you think
where it can go too

00:50:02.330 --> 00:50:04.590 align:middle line:90%
far with the game?

00:50:04.590 --> 00:50:07.870 align:middle line:84%
What's the assumption behind
that question?

00:50:07.870 --> 00:50:12.790 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: There's a really
disturbing news report I was

00:50:12.790 --> 00:50:18.120 align:middle line:84%
reading about this Japanese
game called RapeLay.

00:50:18.120 --> 00:50:22.040 align:middle line:84%
And for me it was clear this is
totally messed up, I can't

00:50:22.040 --> 00:50:24.530 align:middle line:90%
believe this exists.

00:50:24.530 --> 00:50:29.020 align:middle line:84%
But the part that was disturbing
was the defense put

00:50:29.020 --> 00:50:31.580 align:middle line:90%
forth by the developers.

00:50:31.580 --> 00:50:34.810 align:middle line:84%
And they were saying who are you
guys to be telling us this

00:50:34.810 --> 00:50:35.990 align:middle line:90%
is messed up.

00:50:35.990 --> 00:50:38.190 align:middle line:84%
You Americans, you make games
all the time where you have to

00:50:38.190 --> 00:50:39.490 align:middle line:90%
kill people.

00:50:39.490 --> 00:50:43.750 align:middle line:84%
Murder is a much worse
crime than rape.

00:50:43.750 --> 00:50:46.600 align:middle line:84%
Our game is not nearly
as bad as, say, this.

00:50:46.600 --> 00:50:50.690 align:middle line:84%
And I was, wait, no, but
that's more messed up.

00:50:50.690 --> 00:50:52.710 align:middle line:90%
But, what?

00:50:52.710 --> 00:50:55.535 align:middle line:84%
And I don't know what
to think of it.

00:50:55.535 --> 00:50:58.285 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:50:58.285 --> 00:50:58.760 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: What?

00:50:58.760 --> 00:51:02.005 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: In a South Park
episode they address it

00:51:02.005 --> 00:51:04.312 align:middle line:90%
[UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:51:04.312 --> 00:51:07.570 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Actually, just
online on that game in

00:51:07.570 --> 00:51:10.114 align:middle line:84%
particular there was a backlash
that I heard in Japan

00:51:10.114 --> 00:51:14.120 align:middle line:84%
about RapeLay mainly because
the game had been out for

00:51:14.120 --> 00:51:18.360 align:middle line:84%
maybe two years and gone
completely unnoticed until,

00:51:18.360 --> 00:51:21.520 align:middle line:84%
basically, the West heard about
it, American and Europe.

00:51:21.520 --> 00:51:23.960 align:middle line:84%
Because I think it showed up on
Amazon.com or something at

00:51:23.960 --> 00:51:24.820 align:middle line:90%
some point.

00:51:24.820 --> 00:51:26.986 align:middle line:84%
And of course, there was
a gigantic controversy.

00:51:26.986 --> 00:51:29.004 align:middle line:84%
The media blew it
up completely,

00:51:29.004 --> 00:51:30.932 align:middle line:90%
and so then, in Japan--

00:51:30.932 --> 00:51:34.306 align:middle line:84%
was it banned in
Japan, I think?

00:51:34.306 --> 00:51:35.270 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: No.

00:51:35.270 --> 00:51:35.752 align:middle line:90%
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:51:35.752 --> 00:51:37.633 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: But it had been out
for a while and no one had

00:51:37.633 --> 00:51:37.753 align:middle line:90%
paid attention.

00:51:37.753 --> 00:51:39.182 align:middle line:90%
All of a sudden--

00:51:39.182 --> 00:51:41.200 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Apparently Japanese
politicians started cracking

00:51:41.200 --> 00:51:42.730 align:middle line:90%
down on these types of games.

00:51:42.730 --> 00:51:45.270 align:middle line:84%
And a lot of fans got really
angry and they said this is

00:51:45.270 --> 00:51:49.130 align:middle line:84%
just like your Western
Euro-centric view, and why is

00:51:49.130 --> 00:51:51.090 align:middle line:84%
this backlashing onto
us kind of thing.

00:51:51.090 --> 00:51:54.200 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: The only reason why
there was a backlash was

00:51:54.200 --> 00:51:56.470 align:middle line:84%
because of political
attention not

00:51:56.470 --> 00:51:59.880 align:middle line:90%
necessarily because of moral--

00:51:59.880 --> 00:52:02.470 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: A lot of our
moral framework is

00:52:02.470 --> 00:52:05.180 align:middle line:90%
Judeo-Christian in nature.

00:52:05.180 --> 00:52:08.550 align:middle line:84%
I read an entire Wikipedia
article on such things

00:52:08.550 --> 00:52:10.720 align:middle line:90%
afterwards.

00:52:10.720 --> 00:52:15.120 align:middle line:84%
Child pornography, as long as
it doesn't actually show the

00:52:15.120 --> 00:52:18.000 align:middle line:84%
actors, so representations,
animated, 3D, whatever is not

00:52:18.000 --> 00:52:20.640 align:middle line:90%
illegal in Japan.

00:52:20.640 --> 00:52:24.110 align:middle line:84%
And there's no real
political pressure

00:52:24.110 --> 00:52:25.720 align:middle line:90%
either to make it illegal.

00:52:25.720 --> 00:52:28.090 align:middle line:84%
It's just not part of the public
consciousness that it's

00:52:28.090 --> 00:52:31.190 align:middle line:84%
something that needs
to be made illegal.

00:52:31.190 --> 00:52:36.240 align:middle line:84%
And the point is not a weird
point, completely crazy, that

00:52:36.240 --> 00:52:40.580 align:middle line:84%
killing people is probably
the worst crime.

00:52:40.580 --> 00:52:42.980 align:middle line:84%
And the West makes games that
advocate that all the time.

00:52:42.980 --> 00:52:46.312 align:middle line:90%


00:52:46.312 --> 00:52:48.395 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So the question
of [INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:52:48.395 --> 00:52:49.596 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Would there
probably be a radio

00:52:49.596 --> 00:52:52.837 align:middle line:90%
interview with that answer?

00:52:52.837 --> 00:52:56.390 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: In terms of the
question of whether or not a

00:52:56.390 --> 00:52:58.520 align:middle line:84%
game crosses the line first
assumes that there is a line

00:52:58.520 --> 00:52:59.315 align:middle line:90%
to be crossed.

00:52:59.315 --> 00:53:03.710 align:middle line:84%
And second, I guess, my answer
would be yes there is a line

00:53:03.710 --> 00:53:05.140 align:middle line:84%
but that line is basically
whether or not

00:53:05.140 --> 00:53:05.990 align:middle line:90%
people buy the game.

00:53:05.990 --> 00:53:08.120 align:middle line:84%
If people buy the game
the game hasn't

00:53:08.120 --> 00:53:10.052 align:middle line:90%
really crossed the line.

00:53:10.052 --> 00:53:16.850 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So [UNINTELLIGIBLE],
the video reminds me of the

00:53:16.850 --> 00:53:20.440 align:middle line:84%
game of 9/11, where you
are a person on

00:53:20.440 --> 00:53:21.800 align:middle line:90%
one of the top floors--

00:53:21.800 --> 00:53:22.940 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: Oh, and
you could jump.

00:53:22.940 --> 00:53:23.530 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:53:23.530 --> 00:53:25.230 align:middle line:84%
Well, that's the only
way to end the game.

00:53:25.230 --> 00:53:28.340 align:middle line:84%
You can go any way you want
but there's no escape.

00:53:28.340 --> 00:53:33.785 align:middle line:84%
And so the only way to end the
game is to jump out a window.

00:53:33.785 --> 00:53:38.784 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: That had
the same response.

00:53:38.784 --> 00:53:41.510 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I was just going to
say, back on the point of is

00:53:41.510 --> 00:53:42.630 align:middle line:90%
there a line or not?

00:53:42.630 --> 00:53:45.060 align:middle line:84%
I think the line is not
a line on whether it

00:53:45.060 --> 00:53:45.960 align:middle line:90%
becomes OK or not.

00:53:45.960 --> 00:53:46.725 align:middle line:90%
It's a line between--

00:53:46.725 --> 00:53:48.690 align:middle line:84%
I know we mentioned this
before, but-- between

00:53:48.690 --> 00:53:49.940 align:middle line:90%
pornography and art.

00:53:49.940 --> 00:53:52.090 align:middle line:90%


00:53:52.090 --> 00:53:55.350 align:middle line:84%
There's ways to take pictures of
naked human bodies and it's

00:53:55.350 --> 00:53:57.580 align:middle line:84%
artful and people respect
it and all that.

00:53:57.580 --> 00:54:01.653 align:middle line:84%
And there's ways in games to
represent killing and rape and

00:54:01.653 --> 00:54:04.790 align:middle line:84%
all that kind of stuff in a way
that provides a message

00:54:04.790 --> 00:54:07.090 align:middle line:84%
within this framework
that makes it OK.

00:54:07.090 --> 00:54:09.520 align:middle line:90%
And there's games where--

00:54:09.520 --> 00:54:12.390 align:middle line:84%
I think Manhunt is one of
these-- where there's no real

00:54:12.390 --> 00:54:15.780 align:middle line:84%
point other than just displaying
just terrible,

00:54:15.780 --> 00:54:18.940 align:middle line:84%
terrible images for the sake
of displaying that.

00:54:18.940 --> 00:54:22.610 align:middle line:84%
In my mind it's very, very
similar to the discussion of

00:54:22.610 --> 00:54:25.858 align:middle line:84%
is that image pornography
or art?

00:54:25.858 --> 00:54:28.555 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: See, I think that's
the right way to go with this

00:54:28.555 --> 00:54:31.030 align:middle line:90%
kind of a thing in particular.

00:54:31.030 --> 00:54:34.440 align:middle line:84%
I feel that there's this
question of is it doing

00:54:34.440 --> 00:54:36.630 align:middle line:84%
anything meaningful in the game
other than just being

00:54:36.630 --> 00:54:37.880 align:middle line:90%
gratuitous?

00:54:37.880 --> 00:54:39.915 align:middle line:84%
And in this case it's
definitely, yes.

00:54:39.915 --> 00:54:46.480 align:middle line:84%
It progresses the plot, it makes
you think, I guess, a

00:54:46.480 --> 00:54:49.570 align:middle line:84%
bit about the characters
that are involved.

00:54:49.570 --> 00:54:55.120 align:middle line:84%
There's character development
with Makarov here, it's

00:54:55.120 --> 00:54:57.985 align:middle line:84%
showing the shady dealings
of the CIA and all

00:54:57.985 --> 00:54:59.340 align:middle line:90%
this kind of business.

00:54:59.340 --> 00:55:02.870 align:middle line:84%
It's also making a political
statement at the same time.

00:55:02.870 --> 00:55:07.410 align:middle line:84%
So yeah, it's not just nailing
people's eyes to the ceiling

00:55:07.410 --> 00:55:11.622 align:middle line:84%
with a nail gun and
what have you.

00:55:11.622 --> 00:55:13.613 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I was thinking,
are there any games that

00:55:13.613 --> 00:55:13.732 align:middle line:90%
are over the line?

00:55:13.732 --> 00:55:14.650 align:middle line:84%
I could definitely imagine
a game that

00:55:14.650 --> 00:55:16.332 align:middle line:90%
would be over the line.

00:55:16.332 --> 00:55:20.706 align:middle line:84%
You could imagine in RapeLay, or
whatever it's called, there

00:55:20.706 --> 00:55:23.622 align:middle line:84%
were a customizable character
thing that you were

00:55:23.622 --> 00:55:25.274 align:middle line:84%
advertising, rape your
neighbors or

00:55:25.274 --> 00:55:25.566 align:middle line:90%
something like that.

00:55:25.566 --> 00:55:27.510 align:middle line:84%
I would be uncomfortable with
something like that.

00:55:27.510 --> 00:55:30.110 align:middle line:90%


00:55:30.110 --> 00:55:33.380 align:middle line:84%
I almost wanted to say that
there is no line if people buy

00:55:33.380 --> 00:55:37.392 align:middle line:84%
it, but I feel like there are
some things that they would be

00:55:37.392 --> 00:55:39.630 align:middle line:84%
over the line regardless of
whether people buy it or not.

00:55:39.630 --> 00:55:41.860 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: People might even
buy it because they are

00:55:41.860 --> 00:55:43.040 align:middle line:90%
over this line.

00:55:43.040 --> 00:55:44.180 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah, of course.

00:55:44.180 --> 00:55:45.890 align:middle line:90%
That's why you would buy that.

00:55:45.890 --> 00:55:48.250 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: How many of you
have played or seen Super

00:55:48.250 --> 00:55:50.967 align:middle line:90%
Columbine Massacre RPG?

00:55:50.967 --> 00:55:53.352 align:middle line:84%
How many of you have
heard of it?

00:55:53.352 --> 00:55:54.310 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:55:54.310 --> 00:55:57.254 align:middle line:90%
Does that game cross the line?

00:55:57.254 --> 00:55:58.652 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Can somebody
describe this?

00:55:58.652 --> 00:55:59.118 align:middle line:90%
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:55:59.118 --> 00:56:03.390 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I mean, I get the
idea. from the title.

00:56:03.390 --> 00:56:05.750 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: Somebody
want to?

00:56:05.750 --> 00:56:13.085 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: It's kind of a Super
Nintendo US-style RPG.

00:56:13.085 --> 00:56:17.045 align:middle line:84%
It has you walking around in
this sort of Final Fantasy

00:56:17.045 --> 00:56:23.320 align:middle line:84%
with Nintendo era kind of
graphics with quotes and

00:56:23.320 --> 00:56:28.675 align:middle line:84%
dialogue that have been
collected from the actual news

00:56:28.675 --> 00:56:35.435 align:middle line:90%
reports of the [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:56:35.435 --> 00:56:38.900 align:middle line:90%
diaries and stuff like that.

00:56:38.900 --> 00:56:47.645 align:middle line:84%
And yeah, it is largely about
the events leading up to and

00:56:47.645 --> 00:56:48.800 align:middle line:90%
the massacre itself.

00:56:48.800 --> 00:56:52.265 align:middle line:84%
Yes, and you do go around-- and
I can't remember whether

00:56:52.265 --> 00:56:53.255 align:middle line:90%
you actually--

00:56:53.255 --> 00:56:55.235 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: You
do everything.

00:56:55.235 --> 00:56:56.250 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: You shoot
everybody.

00:56:56.250 --> 00:56:58.770 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: You have to
sneak into the school.

00:56:58.770 --> 00:57:03.370 align:middle line:84%
I forget which one you start off
as, it's either Kinkel or

00:57:03.370 --> 00:57:05.250 align:middle line:84%
Harris, but you pick
up your friend.

00:57:05.250 --> 00:57:07.527 align:middle line:84%
It's very RPGish in the
beginning where you're in the

00:57:07.527 --> 00:57:10.470 align:middle line:84%
room because you touch objects
and it gives flashbacks to

00:57:10.470 --> 00:57:13.770 align:middle line:84%
your friendship with this guy
like learning how to make pipe

00:57:13.770 --> 00:57:16.270 align:middle line:84%
bombs behind the convenience
store.

00:57:16.270 --> 00:57:17.550 align:middle line:84%
And you pick him up, you
go to the school.

00:57:17.550 --> 00:57:20.470 align:middle line:84%
You have to sneak in and
plant some bombs.

00:57:20.470 --> 00:57:22.564 align:middle line:84%
And after those go off then you
move through the school

00:57:22.564 --> 00:57:23.560 align:middle line:90%
and you're shooting.

00:57:23.560 --> 00:57:28.042 align:middle line:84%
And it's interesting
[UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:57:28.042 --> 00:57:34.870 align:middle line:84%
I've showed it to a class once
in Ohio, my old school, and

00:57:34.870 --> 00:57:38.050 align:middle line:84%
I'm sneaking into the school,
right, and there are security

00:57:38.050 --> 00:57:40.130 align:middle line:90%
cameras you have to avoid.

00:57:40.130 --> 00:57:43.050 align:middle line:84%
And students, you can't be
seen by them either.

00:57:43.050 --> 00:57:45.760 align:middle line:84%
And there's a point where I'm
trying to manipulate the

00:57:45.760 --> 00:57:48.400 align:middle line:84%
character and everyone
in class is like, oh,

00:57:48.400 --> 00:57:49.590 align:middle line:90%
that way, that way.

00:57:49.590 --> 00:57:52.790 align:middle line:84%
Oh, oh, you blew it, you got
seen, you got kicked out.

00:57:52.790 --> 00:57:55.045 align:middle line:84%
And it was like this moment
of typical gaming,

00:57:55.045 --> 00:57:56.696 align:middle line:90%
like, oh, you did it.

00:57:56.696 --> 00:58:00.300 align:middle line:84%
And then you realize, I got
kicked out of the school

00:58:00.300 --> 00:58:02.068 align:middle line:84%
because I was trying
to plant a bomb.

00:58:02.068 --> 00:58:02.640 align:middle line:90%
And this was based on

00:58:02.640 --> 00:58:03.980 align:middle line:90%
something that really happened.

00:58:03.980 --> 00:58:06.490 align:middle line:84%
And it was that moment of like
you were saying with the

00:58:06.490 --> 00:58:09.560 align:middle line:84%
friendly fire and not sure
who you're shooting.

00:58:09.560 --> 00:58:14.250 align:middle line:84%
And it was like, wow, this is
something that made me think

00:58:14.250 --> 00:58:15.958 align:middle line:84%
about what about all those other
games where I'm happy to

00:58:15.958 --> 00:58:18.802 align:middle line:84%
kill people, and I don't
really think about the

00:58:18.802 --> 00:58:19.276 align:middle line:90%
consequences.

00:58:19.276 --> 00:58:20.698 align:middle line:84%
And this is a game where
you are forced to.

00:58:20.698 --> 00:58:22.790 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: [UNINTELLIGIBLE] a
really interesting parallel in

00:58:22.790 --> 00:58:26.020 align:middle line:84%
a film that I could think of,
The Battle of Algiers.

00:58:26.020 --> 00:58:29.985 align:middle line:84%
I don't know if you've seen this
but there's this scene

00:58:29.985 --> 00:58:36.665 align:middle line:84%
where you are following the
insurgents who are these two

00:58:36.665 --> 00:58:40.940 align:middle line:84%
women who've managed to smuggle
bombs into a cafe they

00:58:40.940 --> 00:58:45.685 align:middle line:84%
think leaders of the civilians
hang out, which is largely

00:58:45.685 --> 00:58:49.680 align:middle line:84%
frequented by the ruling
class French

00:58:49.680 --> 00:58:51.670 align:middle line:90%
section of society there.

00:58:51.670 --> 00:58:55.670 align:middle line:84%
And there's this incredibly
tense scene where they're

00:58:55.670 --> 00:58:59.835 align:middle line:84%
trying to leave their bombs
in the cafe without being

00:58:59.835 --> 00:59:01.767 align:middle line:90%
detected and get out of there.

00:59:01.767 --> 00:59:03.840 align:middle line:84%
And you're like, oh my god,
are they going to be

00:59:03.840 --> 00:59:04.670 align:middle line:90%
able to make it?

00:59:04.670 --> 00:59:07.369 align:middle line:84%
I hope that they can
bomb this cafe.

00:59:07.369 --> 00:59:09.301 align:middle line:90%
What am I thinking?

00:59:09.301 --> 00:59:11.716 align:middle line:90%
This is terrible.

00:59:11.716 --> 00:59:16.546 align:middle line:84%
And you definitely have that
same kind of tension.

00:59:16.546 --> 00:59:19.900 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I feel like people
devote too much emotional

00:59:19.900 --> 00:59:23.910 align:middle line:90%
energy into getting offended.

00:59:23.910 --> 00:59:26.410 align:middle line:84%
They pour their heart
and souls into

00:59:26.410 --> 00:59:28.884 align:middle line:90%
getting offended by things.

00:59:28.884 --> 00:59:34.230 align:middle line:84%
And the line for me is is it
does it advocate and is it

00:59:34.230 --> 00:59:36.540 align:middle line:90%
instructional?

00:59:36.540 --> 00:59:39.140 align:middle line:84%
If it's not either of those two
things it doesn't affect

00:59:39.140 --> 00:59:43.380 align:middle line:84%
me really, and if you really
want to go out and play some

00:59:43.380 --> 00:59:46.620 align:middle line:84%
messed up shit it's none
of my business.

00:59:46.620 --> 00:59:49.796 align:middle line:84%
And my line is not going to be
the same as your line, it's

00:59:49.796 --> 00:59:51.090 align:middle line:84%
not going to be the same
as someone else's line.

00:59:51.090 --> 00:59:54.600 align:middle line:84%
And because there is no human
definable line it really

00:59:54.600 --> 00:59:56.940 align:middle line:84%
scares me that someone
could define a line.

00:59:56.940 --> 01:00:00.820 align:middle line:84%
And since I don't like that idea
I'd rather say that as

01:00:00.820 --> 01:00:04.970 align:middle line:84%
long as it's not like and then
you pull back the safety, and

01:00:04.970 --> 01:00:07.500 align:middle line:84%
here's how you sneak into the
airport, and then here's how

01:00:07.500 --> 01:00:08.510 align:middle line:90%
you shoot this.

01:00:08.510 --> 01:00:10.650 align:middle line:84%
As long as it's not something
like that, and now please go

01:00:10.650 --> 01:00:12.340 align:middle line:84%
do it, here's why you
should because

01:00:12.340 --> 01:00:14.340 align:middle line:90%
America is evil, or whatever.

01:00:14.340 --> 01:00:16.260 align:middle line:84%
That would scare me because I
think that would be increasing

01:00:16.260 --> 01:00:18.445 align:middle line:84%
the chances that someone shoots
me up at an airport.

01:00:18.445 --> 01:00:22.840 align:middle line:84%
But otherwise, because my
attitudes are so liberal and

01:00:22.840 --> 01:00:23.700 align:middle line:84%
someone else's attitude is going
to be so conservative,

01:00:23.700 --> 01:00:25.764 align:middle line:84%
and oh god, someone else's
attitude is going to be even

01:00:25.764 --> 01:00:28.405 align:middle line:84%
more liberal than mine and be OK
with things that I wouldn't

01:00:28.405 --> 01:00:28.820 align:middle line:90%
feel comfortable with.

01:00:28.820 --> 01:00:30.590 align:middle line:84%
If I was allowed to draw a line,
someone else could draw

01:00:30.590 --> 01:00:33.290 align:middle line:84%
that somewhere else, either
before or after, and that

01:00:33.290 --> 01:00:35.160 align:middle line:90%
would just mess everything up.

01:00:35.160 --> 01:00:37.730 align:middle line:84%
And so that's how
I feel about it.

01:00:37.730 --> 01:00:40.760 align:middle line:84%
And I think that the environment
right now kind of

01:00:40.760 --> 01:00:43.775 align:middle line:90%
tends towards that in that--

01:00:43.775 --> 01:00:44.310 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [UNINTELLIGIBLE]?

01:00:44.310 --> 01:00:44.920 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Huh?

01:00:44.920 --> 01:00:46.050 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: The line drawing?

01:00:46.050 --> 01:00:47.570 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah, in that--

01:00:47.570 --> 01:00:49.650 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Depends on
which line drawing?

01:00:49.650 --> 01:00:51.370 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

01:00:51.370 --> 01:00:53.600 align:middle line:84%
People who make games are not
intrinsically, on average,

01:00:53.600 --> 01:00:56.193 align:middle line:84%
really messed up, and so tend
to make stuff that they feel

01:00:56.193 --> 01:00:58.410 align:middle line:90%
generally comfortable with.

01:00:58.410 --> 01:01:02.080 align:middle line:84%
And those who are messed up,
you just draw attention to

01:01:02.080 --> 01:01:05.670 align:middle line:84%
them, really, by giving
them publicity anyway.

01:01:05.670 --> 01:01:07.545 align:middle line:84%
For every person
who says that--

01:01:07.545 --> 01:01:10.840 align:middle line:90%


01:01:10.840 --> 01:01:12.825 align:middle line:84%
Diablo 2 got an M rating
because of violence.

01:01:12.825 --> 01:01:14.950 align:middle line:84%
Now if you look at it today
it's like little sprites

01:01:14.950 --> 01:01:16.400 align:middle line:90%
whacking at each other.

01:01:16.400 --> 01:01:18.090 align:middle line:84%
But that was the
line back then.

01:01:18.090 --> 01:01:22.700 align:middle line:84%
And if that's the line today
then this stuff is absolutely

01:01:22.700 --> 01:01:24.310 align:middle line:84%
horrendous and should
not be disseminated.

01:01:24.310 --> 01:01:26.950 align:middle line:84%
But I think it should be, and
someone else might think what

01:01:26.950 --> 01:01:29.410 align:middle line:90%
I think is OK is not OK.

01:01:29.410 --> 01:01:33.870 align:middle line:84%
It's just going to be what
I think is not OK is OK.

01:01:33.870 --> 01:01:35.238 align:middle line:90%
It's very complicated.

01:01:35.238 --> 01:01:37.433 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: This guy
got death threats.

01:01:37.433 --> 01:01:39.798 align:middle line:90%
Apparently seen this game.

01:01:39.798 --> 01:01:46.635 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: I'd strongly like
to agree with the a lot of

01:01:46.635 --> 01:01:53.240 align:middle line:84%
effort being put into the line
drawing especially the public

01:01:53.240 --> 01:01:57.548 align:middle line:84%
display of, hey, I'm drawing
the line here, mostly by

01:01:57.548 --> 01:02:02.690 align:middle line:84%
politicians but also by people
with some sort of possibly,

01:02:02.690 --> 01:02:06.650 align:middle line:90%
often self-interested agenda.

01:02:06.650 --> 01:02:09.900 align:middle line:84%
And both by the game industry
and by people who

01:02:09.900 --> 01:02:11.770 align:middle line:84%
[UNINTELLIGIBLE] the
game industry.

01:02:11.770 --> 01:02:19.030 align:middle line:84%
It seems like there's more
effort either being used to

01:02:19.030 --> 01:02:23.320 align:middle line:84%
figure out what is offensive and
what is not offensive than

01:02:23.320 --> 01:02:29.770 align:middle line:84%
to discuss the need
that some things

01:02:29.770 --> 01:02:31.190 align:middle line:90%
should be able to offend.

01:02:31.190 --> 01:02:35.430 align:middle line:84%
That we should have stuff that
needs [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

01:02:35.430 --> 01:02:39.912 align:middle line:90%


01:02:39.912 --> 01:02:43.896 align:middle line:84%
There needs to be some stuff
that crosses because maybe the

01:02:43.896 --> 01:02:44.900 align:middle line:90%
line [INAUDIBLE].

01:02:44.900 --> 01:02:47.280 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: And it's a method to
sensitize adults to violence.

01:02:47.280 --> 01:02:49.630 align:middle line:84%
There's so much violence these
days it'd be better if you

01:02:49.630 --> 01:02:51.686 align:middle line:84%
didn't freak out at
every little--

01:02:51.686 --> 01:02:54.674 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I think it's
totally OK to be

01:02:54.674 --> 01:02:55.490 align:middle line:90%
freaked out at things.

01:02:55.490 --> 01:02:56.790 align:middle line:84%
I think that you should
definitely say when you're

01:02:56.790 --> 01:03:00.016 align:middle line:84%
offended by something, and I
think that should be open to

01:03:00.016 --> 01:03:05.860 align:middle line:84%
debate like whether something
is a decent artistic

01:03:05.860 --> 01:03:08.540 align:middle line:84%
expression or
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

01:03:08.540 --> 01:03:12.540 align:middle line:84%
So there's a difference between
staging that you find

01:03:12.540 --> 01:03:15.290 align:middle line:84%
something offensive and
attempting to have these

01:03:15.290 --> 01:03:16.460 align:middle line:84%
things banned or
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

01:03:16.460 --> 01:03:17.546 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: I agree.

01:03:17.546 --> 01:03:21.110 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I'm totally fine with
people wasting words on

01:03:21.110 --> 01:03:22.830 align:middle line:90%
saying whether it's--

01:03:22.830 --> 01:03:24.200 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: No clearly.

01:03:24.200 --> 01:03:26.420 align:middle line:84%
But as long as because you are
offended you think I shouldn't

01:03:26.420 --> 01:03:28.610 align:middle line:84%
be able to look at it,
that's the problem.

01:03:28.610 --> 01:03:29.540 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: OK.

01:03:29.540 --> 01:03:30.250 align:middle line:90%
I think it's--

01:03:30.250 --> 01:03:35.480 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: Or even better, if
by being offended you get to

01:03:35.480 --> 01:03:38.830 align:middle line:90%
now make your point.

01:03:38.830 --> 01:03:41.460 align:middle line:84%
This is why it is offensive
to me and these are

01:03:41.460 --> 01:03:42.730 align:middle line:90%
my reasons for it.

01:03:42.730 --> 01:03:46.110 align:middle line:84%
It might be more informative
and actually better for

01:03:46.110 --> 01:03:49.900 align:middle line:84%
society to get those ideas
out into the open.

01:03:49.900 --> 01:03:52.310 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Suppose you get
offended by proxy?

01:03:52.310 --> 01:03:56.715 align:middle line:84%
It doesn't offend me but I can
see how it offends others.

01:03:56.715 --> 01:03:59.910 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: I think there's
also a conflation of taking

01:03:59.910 --> 01:04:04.310 align:middle line:84%
offense and discomfort because
playing these games might be

01:04:04.310 --> 01:04:06.046 align:middle line:90%
uncomfortable.

01:04:06.046 --> 01:04:10.385 align:middle line:84%
And I don't think that's
necessarily a bad thing if it

01:04:10.385 --> 01:04:13.110 align:middle line:84%
makes you think about, like
[UNINTELLIGIBLE] was saying,

01:04:13.110 --> 01:04:15.940 align:middle line:84%
or like [UNINTELLIGIBLE] game,
this Super Columbine Massacre,

01:04:15.940 --> 01:04:17.740 align:middle line:84%
if it makes you think about
things in a different way, or

01:04:17.740 --> 01:04:18.650 align:middle line:90%
like that film.

01:04:18.650 --> 01:04:21.815 align:middle line:84%
And especially games have the
ability to provoke that in you

01:04:21.815 --> 01:04:23.430 align:middle line:84%
to make you think because
you're the one.

01:04:23.430 --> 01:04:24.720 align:middle line:84%
Especially these games
that force you

01:04:24.720 --> 01:04:25.640 align:middle line:90%
to do certain things.

01:04:25.640 --> 01:04:28.546 align:middle line:84%
It's like, well, I don't even
think about is there a game

01:04:28.546 --> 01:04:31.460 align:middle line:84%
that you absolutely refuse
to play because there was

01:04:31.460 --> 01:04:33.670 align:middle line:84%
something in it that you just
really did not want to do?

01:04:33.670 --> 01:04:36.200 align:middle line:90%


01:04:36.200 --> 01:04:37.100 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: That's fine.

01:04:37.100 --> 01:04:38.600 align:middle line:90%
GUEST SPEAKER: OK?

01:04:38.600 --> 01:04:40.100 align:middle line:90%
I think we'll end there.

01:04:40.100 --> 01:04:41.350 align:middle line:90%